joining us in the studio ian murray  shadow secretary of state for scotland  ms murray good to see you this morning  um well let's start with the northern  ireland protocol according to to jacob  rees-mogg you know we didn't see these  problems coming there's scope within uh  the agreement to renegotiate i mean on  that he's got a point doesn't he well  everyone did see this coming because  they said it was an oven ready deal and  we said if you put a border in the irish  they'll be inevitably problems because  the border between the great britain the  united kingdom and the eu is in ireland  now and and that was always going to  cause  difficulties but to rip up something  that they signed only a couple of years  ago or to threaten to rip up it's not  really resolving the problem and as  kirsten has consistently said yes they  went down the route of getting brexit  done but they've got to try and make  brexit work and it doesn't look as if  they're willing to do so but the  european union could unilaterally decide  not to insist on checks on everything  that's going across at the irc couldn't  they well they've offered various  solutions already including on  veterinary passports which the  government are seemingly rejecting so  there are things on the table this has  to happen through negotiation our  relationship post european union  membership was never going to be a  simple straightforward one because  nobody had done this before and  therefore it takes two parties to come  together to negotiate at what should be  a relatively simple negotiation because  both both parties are in roughly the  same place in terms of wanting trade to  happen but just specifically mr  rees-mogg is correct when he says that  there is scope in what was agreed back  in 2019 for aspects to be renegotiated  well let's have it done then let's get  the european union and the uk government  together but it seems to me that this  current government and we're interested  in picking fights with the eu rather  than trying to resolve it and the only  thing only people that suffer here are  the people of northern ireland because  they're in a situation now where they're  stuck with this border in the iric a uk  government and threatening the eu with  ripping up the protocol and the eu  threatening our trade work can you  imagine what would happen to the cost of  living crisis in northern ireland had we  ended up in a trade war with the  european union let's get both uh parties  round the table and let's get this  resolved so you presumably then think  that sir jeffrey donaldson head of the  yeah the big big obviously big figure in  northern irish politics but in his role  within the tup you think he's acting  entirely properly and you know  essentially holding installment to  ransom until he gets what he wants on  the protocol well the good friday  agreement lays out the processes for  setting up a power sharing agreement  pretty clearly and that is that the  first minister of northern ireland comes  from the party who wins the most seats  and there has to be a per share in  agreement for all the communities  represented in northern ireland and  that's what should happen everyone  should do this but if but it's not so do  you agree with it that do you agree with  what sir jeffrey is doing no absolutely  not i mean it's quite clear that the  power sharing agreement has to be put in  place the good friday agreement is clear  about what should happen that is endured  now since it was signed back in the  mid-1990s so therefore that has to be  followed through we want to see all  communities represented in a persian  agreement wants torment to work and that  has to come together to hold any sight  to ransom in these kinds of talks it  just makes things much more difficult  people have to come together and find  solutions to these issues  the problem is is that we have had  decade after decade of suggesting that  people need to come together in northern  ireland we we had a functioning  installment for for a good length of  time it's difficult to see a way back to  having a functional uh having a  functional installment of a functional  northern ireland assembly absent the  government  doing the unthinkable you know in your  mind and actually sorting out the  problems that there are with the  protocol in the short-term future it's  not going to happen but that has to  happen that's the only option that's on  the table because the good friday  agreement sets out quite clearly both  the legal and political and  constitutional ways in which northern  ireland assembly it comes together  that's now not happening because one  party to that agreement's decided not to  play ball now we've got the election of  speaker today we'll see whether or not  the dup actually vote for that speaker  from sir jeffrey this morning that they  will not be well if they don't  participate then the northern ireland  assembly therefore can't progress in the  way in which it should and therefore  we'll be in back in another election  again the people of northern ireland  have voted that's the way they have  voted we should respect that and the key  thing about the good friday agreement is  all communities have to be represented  in storm and that's the way it has to  work in order to keep those voices  together to keep peace in northern  ireland and to keep northern ireland  functioning as a democratic party  democratic state house sanguine how  comfortable are you with sinn fein that  a nationalist party a party that overtly  wants to see the reunification of  ireland is the largest party installment  right now well that's what the people of  northern ireland have voted for we need  to respect  that vote yeah but you can tell me how  otherwise you feel about it well i think  we're all uncomfortable with the  situation in northern ireland in terms  of what's happening with the protocol in  terms of what's happening with the  standoff  that's threatening the good friday  agreement everyone said and there'll be  column inches the the the length of  northern ireland and broadcast packages  um out there that shows that everyone  said that what was being proposed in  terms of the protocol may threaten the  good friday agreement now what we're  seeing now is a good friday agreement  being challenged because those steps to  put in place of government are not being  followed through  and therefore everything that was said  about the good federal government and  now seems to be coming to pass it's  really incumbent on the parties in  northern ireland and the uk government  to come together and resolve these  problems what about the big kind of  government announcement actually  pre-announcement would be better given  there aren't very very many details  about it that we will within a couple of  years see a fifth of the civil service  axed what's your view  well this has come out of nowhere the  prime minister challenged his cabinet to  come up with  ideas to deal with the cost of living  crisis it seems the only things they can  come up with is  emoting your car once every two years or  buying the cheap brand foods and  supermarkets refer to the government  this was in the spending review in 2021  but it was half the amount that they  were going for i think it was 45 000  they were looking for back then so it  has been around they've just doubled but  it'll take a long time obviously for  that to feed through and i think the  civil service union's absolutely correct  the government going to have to explain  what the government will no longer do  and you don't have to look very far to  see there's huge problems in the  passport office every mp's inbox will be  full of that at the moment the dvla has  huge problems there's problems in the  home office a person visa and asylum  applications every single department has  huge pressures on it so they'll have to  explain what this means and what it  would mean for government in this video  basically they say 38 000 leave the  civil service every year that plus a you  know a hiring freeze  job done  yes but there's still a consequence of  what the government has to be able to  achieve and has to be able to do you  can't just look at this as a number and  say well the government will stop doing  x y and z they have to lay out what that  actually means and don't forget it is  saving three and a half billion pounds  according to the government's figures  but they're wasting 4.3 billion pounds  on writing off in fraud there was tens  of billions wasted in terms of covert  and ppe contracts so this is a  government of wastage and now they're  turning to the civil service who keep  the government uh operating to say that  they're going to take the take the hits  three and a half billion pounds i mean  it's a lot of money and well rachel  reeves the shadow chancellor's set out  that a future labour government would  have an office of value for money and  look at every single penny of government  spending to make sure it's efficient and  make sure it's doing what it needs to do  in terms of delivering for the public  that's a very key policy of a future  labour government but it seems to me  that this government at this moment are  just flailing around looking for  anything to  try and resolve the cost of living  crisis and frankly the bottom line of  all that is they're doing very little  and people are seeing those huge bills  they're seeing a massive tax hike from  this government and they're wondering  what the government are going to do to  try and help them pay their bills just  before we let you go just a word on uh  evgeny lebedev or lord levidev as i  should call him despite the instruction  from the house of commons the government  is not going to be releasing that  information  what can the house of commons do about  that  well parliament has voted for that  information to be released and the  government are refusing to release it  this is admired and a whole host of  cronyism and the public will be cynical  about why the government aren't  releasing that information when the  parliament has voted for it to be  released this is a huge issue in terms  of the credibility of the prime minister  he had national security advice upon  lord  well we'll just need to bring it back to  parliament we will vote again to try and  get this um  information released and i think maybe  the speaker of the house of commons  should take a role here and say to the  government luke there's been a vote a  democratic vote in parliament you've  been asked to do something you're not  doing it the public will come to the  conclusion if they won't give us this  information and release this information  then they've got something to hide we  quite clearly think there is something  to hide and the public deserve the truth  yeah i suspect the speaker may well be  getting dragged into this a little bit  later on today um ian murray great to  have you in the studio thanks for being  with us

